steps.body:
When sales start to fall, a business has a choice: let the product die, or use an **extension strategy** to lengthen its life.

The common ones are worth knowing by name:

- **Advertising**, to remind people the product exists;
- **Reducing the price**, to keep it competitive;
- **Adding value**, such as new features or better service;
- **New packaging or a new look**, so it feels current;
- **Finding a new market**, such as selling abroad or targeting a different age group;
- **Updating the product**, releasing a new version.

An extension strategy is a **response to decline**, so a question mentioning falling sales is usually inviting one.

Be careful with price cuts. They do lift sales, but every unit now earns less, so total profit can fall even as sales rise. That trade-off is exactly what a *justify* question wants you to weigh.

**Promotion** is how a business tells customers about a product and persuades them to buy. The methods Edexcel names are:

- **Advertising**, paid messages in the media;
- **Sponsorship**, paying to be associated with an event or team;
- **Product trials**, letting people try it free or cheaply;
- **Special offers**, such as two-for-one or a discount;
- **Branding**, building a name that customers recognise and trust.

The skill being tested is **matching the method to the market segment**. The right answer is rarely "advertise more".

- A new food product suits **product trials**, because the barrier is that people have never tasted it.
- A sports drink suits **sponsorship** of a team, because it reaches exactly the people who might buy it.
- A supermarket suits **special offers**, because its customers are price-sensitive and buy weekly.
- A luxury brand avoids **special offers** altogether, because discounting damages the image it is selling.

That last one matters. For some businesses a promotion method is not just less effective but actively harmful.
